# Dual-band simultaneous lasing in MOFs single crystals with Fabry-Perot microcavities

State Key Laboratory of Silicon Materials, Cyrus Tang Center for Sensor Materials and Applications, School of Materials Science and Engineering, Zhejiang University, Hangzhou 310027, China
Multi-band microlasers based on single microcrystalline materials with Fabry-Perot (F-P) cavities are critically and technologically essential. Here, we demonstrate simultaneous dual-band lasing output (615 and $685\mathrm{nm}$ ) in metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) and organic dyes hybrid single crystals, which support F-P resonances. Through a two-step assembly strategy, two different types of cationic pyridinium hemicyanine dye molecules can be encapsulated into the channel pores of anionic bioMOF-1-2Me successfully. In addition, the employment of the host-guest system significantly increases the dye loading, enhances luminescent efficiency, and diminishes the aggregation-caused quenching (ACQ) effect in the resultant MOFs/dye composites. This finding not only combines the characteristic of MOFs materials with excellent luminescent properties of organic dyes, but also points out a simple and promising strategy to design multi-band microlasers based on F-P mechanism, opening a low-cost avenue for the rational design of miniaturized lasers in the future.
dual-band lasing, metal-organic frameworks, organic dye, Fabry-Perot cavity
Citation: Li H, He H, Yu J, Cui Y, Yang Y, Qian G. Dual-band simultaneous lasing in MOFs single crystals with Fabry-Perot microcavities. Sc ChinaChem, 2019, 62, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11426-019-9485-4
## 1 Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have been emerged as one of the most active research fields among scientific community [1–8], especially as a powerful platform for various photonic materials [9–12]. Such porous materials have been demonstrated to be an excellent host matrix for fabricating solid-state microlasers with high performance [13–15]. The regular external morphologies and smooth surfaces of MOFs crystals offer the natural resonant cavities, providing the promise in constructing microlasers. Encapsulating photonic active molecules, such as organic dyes, into MOFs pores is a very important strategy to enlarge the function for the porous characteristic to homogeneously disperse the gain media and minimize the aggregationcaused quenching (ACQ) effects [16]. Further, the framework structure facilitates the loading of considerably different types of organic dyes, providing a possibility of simultaneous multi-band lasing and photoluminescence [17,18].
Fabry-Perot (F-P) cavity is one of the most mature lasers configurations with advantages such as simple fabrication, high efficiency, low threshold, good beam quality and controllable mode spacing [19]. The light rebounds between the end facets, reflecting the stimulated emission to provide the necessary feedback for lasing action. Multi-band or multiwavelength lasers with F-P cavities have drawn great attention for tremendous potential applications, including fullcolor laser imaging, biological inspection and chemical sensing [20–24]. However, traditional method to realize such lasers needs to combine several individual elements and it still suffers from complex fabrication, high cost and relatively large device footprints [25,26]. The development of solid-state microlaser is an effective way to produce multiple lasers of different wavelengths on the same platform. Reflective mirrors or extra fabrications are usually required in F-P modes but it might be inappropriate and challenging for achieving solid-state microlasers. Recently, studies on F-P microcavities are focused on semiconductor nano-structures. But such types of materials are seldomly reported on multiband lasers, which might be due to the difficulty of assembling distinct heterostructures or composition-graded nanostructures into a F-P microcavity with the usual epitaxial growth [27–31].
Considering the challenges mentioned above, MOFs materials are pretty promising to realize such type of microlasers via host-guest system. We synthesized a dual-band emission microlaser based on the MOFs/dye composites. Columnar MOFs crystals can be naturally utilized as the F-P miorocavities and the mode spacing can be controlled by adjusting the crystal sizes. Two kinds of cationic pyridinium hemicyanine dyes were selected as guests to be encapsulated into the anionic framework. Dual-band lasing emissions, centered at 615 and $685\mathrm{nm}$ respectively, were achieved in the as-prepared single crystals under the excitation at $532\mathrm{nm}$ . The wavelengths of 615 and $685\mathrm{nm}$ belong to the red and near infrared (NIR-I) regions, respectively, which have potentials for biological applications, especially for invivo imaging and low-level laser therapy [32,33].
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Materials and general methods
Luminescent dyes, 4- $[p$ -(dimethylamino) styryl]-1-methylpyridinium (DSM) and 4- $(1E,3E)$ -4-(4(dimethylamino) phenyl) buta-1,3-dien-1-yl)-1-methylpyridinium (DABP), were synthesized according to our previously reported procedures [13]. The ligand $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -dimethyl-[1,1′-biphenyl]- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ - dicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{\cdotH_{2}D M B P D C)}$ and other reagents were purchased of reagent grades without further purification. For material characterization, the powder $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction (PXRD) data were collected on an X’Pert PRO diffractometer $\mathrm{{Cu}\ K a}$ radiation $\begin{array}{r}{(\lambda{=}1.542\mathrm{~}\mathrm{\AA}),}\end{array}$ , room temperature, in the range of $2\theta{=}5^{\circ}{-}50^{\circ}$ ). Thermogravimetric analyses (TGA) data were conducted with a Netzsch TG209F3 heated from room temperature to $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under nitrogen atmosphere with the heating rate of $10\ \mathrm{{^circC/min}}$ . The excitation and emission spectra were taken with a Hitachi F4600 fluorescence spectrometer (Japan) at room temperature. The fluorescence lifetime experiments were performed by the time-correlated single-photon counting (TCSPC) system on a Horiba Jobin Deltaflex fluorescence spectrometer. For the optically pumped lasing measurements, an optical parametric amplifier (Spirit-OPA $\cdot^{+}$ Spirit-OPA-UV3, Newport Corporation, USA) was pumped by a fully automated ultrafast laser system (Spirit One 1040-8, 8 W at $1040\mathrm{nm}$ , Newport Corporation, USA), which was used for generating the excitation pulse $(1\mathrm{kHz},532\mathrm{nm}$ , pulse width ${<}400$ fs). The laser beam was focused on the crystals kept on a glass plate, with a $10\times$ microscope object (numerical aperture ${\boldsymbol{\mathbf{\mathit{\sigma}}}}=0.40{\mathrm{\mathit{\sigma}}}$ ) in an Olympus IX71 microscope system. The photoluminescence from the sample was collected by the fiber optic spectrometer (PG2000-Pro, Ideaoptics Instruments).
## 2.2 Preparation of bio-MOF-1-2Me
The synthesis method of bio-MOF-1-2Me was carried out according to our previous work with minor modifications [34]. Zinc nitrate hexahydrate $200~\mathrm{mg}$ , $0.67\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{\Omega},$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{DMBPDC}$ $38.9\mathrm{mg}$ , 0.14 mmol), and adenine ( $\mathrm{14.9\mg}$ , $0.11\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{\Omega},$ ) were dissolved in the solution of $N,N$ -dimethylformamide (DMF, $15\mathrm{mL}$ ), water $(0.9\mathrm{mL})$ and nitric acid $(0.04\mathrm{mL})$ . Colorless rod-like crystals were obtained after keeping the solution in a $20~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon-lined stainlesssteel bomb at $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $36\mathrm{h}$ . Bio-MOF-1-2Me crystals were collected by filtration, washed by DMF and ethanol (EtOH) for several times, dried in air.
## 2.3 Preparation of dye-encapsulated MOFs
Synthesis of DSM $@$ bio-MOF-1-2Me and DABP $@$ bioMOF-1-2Me: The bio-MOF-1-2Me crystals were immersed in the solution of DSM solution with the concentration of $500~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ in DMF at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $5\mathrm{d}$ to yield DSM@bio-MOF-1- 2Me. Subsequently, colored crystals were filtered off, washed with DMF and EtOH for several times in order to remove residual dye from the surface, and then dried in air. The preparation method of DABP $@$ bio-MOF-1-2Me is the same as DSM $@$ bio-MOF-1-2Me.
Synthesis of DSM&DABP $@$ bio-MOF-1-2Me: Colorless bio-MOF-1-2Me crystals were immersed in the DABP solution with the concentration of $500~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $5\mathrm{d}$ to reach saturation and filtered with DMF and EtOH for several times. After dried in air, DABP $@$ bio-MOF-1-2Me crystals were immersed in the $500~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ DSM solution at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $5\mathrm{d}$ . Crystals were collected by filtration, washed with DMF and EtOH and then dried in air.
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Synthesis and structure
Bio-MOF-1-2Me was selected as the host matrix in this work and the single crystals were synthesized through a solvothermal reaction. PXRD results (Figure 1(e)) suggest that the structure of bio-MOF-1-2Me $\mathrm{(Zn_{8}(a d)_{4}(D M B P D C)_{6}O}$ · $2\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ is isoreticular to bio-MOF-1 $\mathrm{(Zn_{8}(A d)_{4}}$ $\mathrm{(BPDC)_{6}O\cdot2M e_{2}N H_{2}}.$ , $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Ad}=$ adeninate, BPDC=biphenyldicarboxylate) because of the same diffraction peak positions in lower angle area according to our previous work [34,35]. This type of anionic MOFs is composed of one-dimensional (1-D) channels along the $c$ axis, which are occupied by $\mathrm{{Me}}_{2}\mathrm{{NH}}_{2}$ cations. Through studying PXRD of the crystals placed in different environments, bio-MOF-1-2Me demonstrated great stability, not only in the air, but also in the common solvents such as water, EtOH and DMF (Figure S3, Supporting Information online) which might be attributed to the sturdy SBUs and hydrophobic methyl groups on the organic ligands. Therefore, the smooth surfaces and complete structures could be retained in the experimental environment [36]. As shown in Figure S6(a) (Supporting Information online), the crystals of bio-MOF-1-2Me exhibit blue emission derived from the $\mathrm{H_{2}D M B P D C}$ linkers under the excitation at $365\mathrm{nm}$ .
Cationic pyridinium hemicyanine dyes, DSM and DABP, were selected as the guests [37,38]. The space filling model structures showed the width of ${\sim}0.61$ and ${\sim}0.81\ \mathrm{nm}$ for DSM and DABP molecules, respectively (Figures S1 and S2). Fresh bio-MOF-1-2Me crystals with the same mass were immersed in two classes of cationic dye solutions (DSM in DMF or DABP in DMF) with the initial concentration of $500~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ to synthesize MOFs/dye composites. The microscopic images of different as-prepared crystals display different colors, red for DSM@MOFs (Figure 1(b)) and purple for DABP $@$ MOFs (Figure 1(d)). Owing to the strong electrostatic interactions, the anionic framework structure could incorporate target cationic dye molecules from their solution into the channels and MOFs/dye hybrid solid-state crystals could be synthesized successfully.
The dye uptake was determined by comparing the difference in dye concentration between initial solution and residual solution through ultraviolet-vis spectroscopy (Figure S9). As a result, the DSM concentration in the DSM@bioMOF-1-2Me composite is calculated to be $1.70\mathrm{wt\%}$ . The DABP concentration in the DABP $@$ bio-MOF-1-2Me is about $0.80\mathrm{wt\%}$ . The uptake of DABP is much less than that of DSM in pure bio-MOF-1-2Me crystals under the same experimental conditions, possibly due to the larger molecular dimension. Considering the actual load of the two dye molecules, we chose to use a two-step assembly strategy to synthesize DSM&DABP $@$ bio-MOF-1-2Me in order to ensure that both DSM and DABP were sufficiently loaded. The specific steps are as follows: (1) DABP was introduced into the channel pores via the ion-exchange process to form DABP@bio-MOF-1-2Me; (2) the as-prepared DABP@bioMOF-1-2Me crystals were immersed into the solution of DSM up to saturation, forming single crystals of DSM&DABP $@$ bio-MOF-1-2Me. The details are shown in the Experimental Section. The dye uptake was determined by the same method, shown in Figure S9. According to the results of PXRD and the microscopy images of crystals shown in Figure 1(c, e), the introduction of both DSM and DABP rarely affects the crystallinity of bio-MOF-1-2Me and the crystals maintain smooth morphologies.
## 3.2 Photoluminescence properties
Several isolated bio-MOF-1-2Me microcrystals were selected to demonstrate the process of dye molecules diffusing into the colorless crystals for different periods of time. The fluorescence microscopic images are shown in Figure $\mathrm{S7(a-}$ d) for DSM@bio-MOF-1-2Me and Figure S7(e–h) for DABP@bio-MOF-1-2Me. As the dye trapped into the host matrix, the crystals exhibit stronger and brighter emission than the surrounding solution environment. At the beginning, both end regions of the crystal turned to red under the excitation at $470\mathrm{nm}$ . With the extension of immersing time, the red emission regions gradually extended along the longitudinal direction of the crystal from the ends to the middle. The entire MOFs crystals displayed bright emission after bio-MOF-1- 2Me crystals immersed in DSM solution or in DABP solution for $24\mathrm{h}$ . The result implies that the ion-exchange process occurs through the diffusion of cationic dye molecules along the 1-D channel pores of bio-MOF-1-2Me.
To verify the enhancement of fluorescence, we compared DSM (or DABP) solution $(100~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ in DMF, powders and DSM (or DABP) $@$ bio-MOF-1-2Me synthesized in $100\upmu\mathrm{M}$ solution. As for DSM (Figure 2(a)), under the excitation at ${500}\mathrm{nm}$ , the solution and powder display very weak emission centered at 610 and $656\mathrm{nm}$ , with a low photoluminescence quantum yield (PLQY) of $1.63\%$ and $0.61\%$ , respectively. In contrast, DSM@bio-MOF-1-2Me exhibits a much stronger emission from around $600\mathrm{nm}$ with a much higher PLQY of $45.23\%$ . Compared with DSM@bio-MOF-1-2Me, a red shifted emission at around $680\mathrm{nm}$ could be observed in DABP@bio-MOF-1-2Me, as shown in Figure 2(b), which could be ascribed to the extension of longer conjugation lengths in DABP molecule. The emission intensities of the DABP solution and powder have been sustained at extremely low levels with the PLQY of $0.94\%$ and $0.07\%$ , while the value of DABP@bio-MOF-1-2Me is $36.37\%$ . The increasing emission intensity of MOFs/dye composites might be due to spatial confinement of 1-D channels, where the dye molecules could be arranged and separated. Thus, intramolecular torsional motion of different dye molecules could be restricted and the ACQ effects could be avoided greatly in the porous framework structure. Similarly, we soaked the bio-MOF-1-2Me crystals in a solution containing $100~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ DSM and $100\upmu\mathrm{M}$ DABP and the crystals absorbed almost all the dye molecules into the pores. While introducing both DSM and DABP molecules into the bio-MOF-1- 2Me crystals simultaneously, the PLQY still presents a significant increase against the mixed solution for $38.48\%$ versus $1.15\%$ according to Figure 2(c).
The excitation and emission wavelengths of DSM and DABP exist a certain overlap, resulting in the formation of the donor-acceptor system in the DSM&DABP@bio-MOF1-2Me single crystals. The energy transfer in DSM&DABP $@$ bio-MOF-1-2Me was studied by the time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) measurements through detecting the fluorescence lifetime at $590\mathrm{nm}$ in DSM@MOFs, $720\mathrm{nm}$ in DABP $@$ MOFs, 590 and $720\mathrm{nm}$ in DSM&DABP $@$ MOFs individually. As shown in Figure 2(d), it is easy to observe that a dramatical decrease of fluorescence lifetime at $590\mathrm{nm}$ from 2.9 to 1.9 ns and a faint increase at $720\mathrm{nm}$ from 2.9 to 3.1 ns. The apparent inconsistency in fluorescence lifetime of dye loaded in different chemical micro-environment is mainly caused by Forster resonance energy transfer (FRET) [39,40]. The excitation energy from an excited donor is nonradiatively transferred to a proximal ground-state acceptor. The energy transfer efficiency $\eta_{\mathrm{FRET}}$ can be calculated by [41]
$$
\eta_{\mathrm{FRET}}=1-\tau_{\mathrm{D}}/\tau_{\mathrm{0D}}
$$
where $\tau_{0\mathrm{D}}$ and $\tau_{\mathrm{D}}$ are the lifetimes of the donor in the absence and presence of the acceptor, respectively. After calculation, the value of $\eta_{\mathrm{FRET}}$ in this system is approximately $31\%$ , which is determined by factors such as the spectral overlap and the distance between donors and acceptors [42–44]. Thus, deliberate and rational design on the dye pairs in MOFs microlasers is essential to control any energy transfer process involved and obtain expected performances.
## 3.3 Single-band lasing properties
For dye $@$ bio-MOF-1-2Me, enhanced fluorescence and high PLQY are beneficial for the achievement of optical gain and amplification. Here, we chose the bio-MOF-1-2Me crystals soaked in dye solution with a concentration of $500~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ to study the lasing properties. Optically pumped lasing measurements were performed on a home-built photoluminescence system. A laser beam at $532\mathrm{nm}$ directed from a femtosecond optical parametric amplifier (OPA) was used to pump the single crystals at room temperature. When the femtosecond pulse laser beam was focused at the center of the MOFs single crystal, bright spot was observed on the excited area relative to the other parts of the crystals (inset of Figure 3(c)). Figure 3(a) illustrates the emission intensity dependence on the pump density by recording a series of emission curves on a high-resolution fiber optic spectrometer. At a low pump density $(105~\upmu\mathrm{J/cm}^{2})$ , a broad and weak spontaneous emission is exhibited according to the photoluminescence (PL) spectra. As the pump density increases to $338~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{J/cm}}^{2}$ , the light emission becomes stronger and develops as a set of sharp peaks centered at $615\mathrm{nm}$ gradually. The curve in the inset of Figure 3(a) shows an obvious knee behavior, which is composed of two fitted linear beelines for the spontaneous emission and lasing regime. A single pulse density threshold $(E_{\mathrm{th}})$ of ${\sim}251~\mathrm{\upmuJ/cm}^{2}$ is estimated from the intersection of the two lines. The fullwidth at half-maximum (FWHM) has a sharp drop from about 60.1 to $1.07\mathrm{nm}$ near the threshold.
Figure 3(b) reveals the emission spectra under different pump densities obtained from single DABP $@$ bio-MOF-1- 2Me crystal. Similar to DSM@bio-MOF-1-2Me, by increasing the pump density, the emission spectra centered at around $680\mathrm{nm}$ begins to narrow, while the peak intensity increases more rapidly. Beyond the pump density of the threshold value, several spikes appear and become stronger gradually. FWHM has a sudden change around the threshold from nearly 75.2 to $0.90\mathrm{nm}$ . The nonlinear relationship of emission intensity versus pump density has been recorded and the threshold is approximately $351~\upmu\mathrm{J/cm}^{2}$ after partition linear fitting (the inset of Figure 3(b)). The existence of splitting peaks and thresholds from DSM $@$ bio-MOF-1-2Me and DABP@bio-MOF-1-2Me confirms that the lasing behavior has occurred in the hybrid crystals. Pure dye crystals were investigated under the same pump source but no lasing behaviors have been observed because the ACQ effects are indeed severe (Figure S11), causing excessive optical loss. MOFs/dye crystals significantly reduce ACQ effects and effectively form an optical feedback mechanism.
## 3.4 Dual-band lasing properties
Dual-band lasing study upon DSM&DABP@bio-MOF-1- 2Me was carried out under the same testing conditions. When the excitation laser fluence was increased above $2.11\mathrm{mJ/cm}^{2}$ , two series of narrow spikes evolved located at 615 and $685\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively (Figure 3(c)). Figure 3(d) depicts the emission intensity and the FWHM at 615 and $685\mathrm{nm}$ as a function of the pump fluence, showing two thresholds, 1.70 and $1.80\mathrm{mJ}/\mathrm{cm}^{\dot{2}}$ corresponding to the emission of DSM and DABP. A possible reason for the increment on thresholds may be ascribed to the doping of two types of dye molecules in different spectral regions, which increases the longitudinal mode competition. Thus, the elaborate design of composition and concentration for different dyes is critical to balancing the relationship between threshold and bandwidth.
Due to the significant difference of the refractive index between the MOFs crystal and external environment, the smooth top and bottom facets of rectangular bio-MOF-1- 2Me crystals could act as the mirrors of a Fabry-Perot cavity.
Several DSM&DABP $@$ bio-MOF-1-2Me crystals with different thicknesses were measured to study the relationship between the resonance modes and the sizes of the MOFs microcavities under the condition of dual-band lasing. Figure 4(a–c) shows the microscopy images of single crystals with the thickness of 10, 26 and $43\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , respectively and red light emits from the crystals under the excitation at $470\mathrm{nm}$ . Figure 4(d) exhibits the corresponding lasing spectra at the pump density above the lasing threshold. Dual-band lasing could be easily distinguished centered at around 615 and $685\mathrm{nm}$ for DSM and DABP molecules in bio-MOF-1-2Me. The mode spacing changes markedly with the thickness of single crystals and the value can be obtained by the corresponding lasing spectra (details shown as Figure S12).
Inductively, it can be seen that the mode spacing $(\Delta\lambda_{\mathrm{s}})$ between adjacent lasing modes decreases with the increase of the thickness towards bio-MOF-1-2Me single crystals. The mode spacing $\Delta\lambda_{\mathrm{s}}$ of F-P mode mechanism is defined by [45]
$$
\Delta\lambda_{\mathrm{s}}=\lambda^{2}/2n_{\mathrm{g}}L
$$
where $L$ is the cavity length as well as the thickness of bioMOF-1-2Me crystal in this work, $\lambda$ is the light wavelength and $n_{\mathrm{g}}$ is the group refractive index. Figure 4(e) summarizes that in both of the two lasing emission regions centered at 615 and $685\mathrm{nm}$ respectively, linear relationships between the measured $\Delta\lambda_{\mathrm{s}}$ and $1/L$ can be revealed, which is in good agreement with Eq. (2) on the basis of the fitting results. Specifically, the group refractive index $n_{\mathrm{g}}{\approx}2.39$ and $n_{\mathrm{g}}{\approx}2.41$ were obtained after calculation and fitting, which were almost identical. According to the results, it confirms that the opposing facets of the bio-MOF-1-2Me single crystals can be used as the reflectors of F-P cavity in order to provide suitable optical confinement.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, we created a novel way to achieve simultaneous dual-band lasing in the single metal-organic frameworks crystals with Fabry-Perot microcavities. Due to the energetically favorable interactions between MOFs and dye molecules, two types of cationic pyridinium hemicyanine dye DSM and DABP were confined in nano-channels of anionic bio-MOF-1-2Me successfully. Consequently, this strategy can significantly increase loading concentration of various dyes, minimize the ACQ effects and enhance luminescent efficiency with relatively low energy transfer efficiency. The opposite facets of the rectangular single bioMOF-1-2Me crystal could act as the reflectors of a F-P cavity. Moreover, through the two-step synthesis method, dual-band lasing at 615 and $685\mathrm{nm}$ simultaneously could be achieved in a single microcrystal with a relatively low threshold under the same pump source. These results provide a new solution for organic dye system to create simultaneous multi-band lasing emission based on F-P microcavities. It may open up a new route to obtain multi-wavelength lasing emission in single MOFs microcrystals, presenting a promising solid-state microlaser for optoelectronic and biomedical applications in the future.
